---
import GuideLayout from '../../components/GuideLayout.astro';

const title = 'How to Set Up 2FA on Zoom (Authenticator App)';
const description =
  'Turn on Zoom two-step verification with an Authentication App, then generate live 6-digit TOTP codes in your browser and save the single-use recovery codes.';
---

<GuideLayout
  serviceName="Zoom"
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/guides/zoom/"
  datePublished="2026-11-18"
  dateModified="2026-11-23"
  lastReviewed="2026-11-23"
  sources={[
    {
      label: 'Zoom Support — Managing two-step verification',
      href: 'https://support.zoom.com/hc/en/article?id=zm_kb&sysparm_article=KB0066054',
      kind: 'official',
    },
    {
      label: 'Zoom Support — Changing account security settings',
      href: 'https://support.zoom.com/hc/en/article?id=zm_kb&sysparm_article=KB0064901',
      kind: 'official',
    },
    {
      label: 'RFC 6238 — TOTP: Time-Based One-Time Password Algorithm',
      href: 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6238',
      kind: 'independent',
    },
  ]}
  intro="Zoom calls it two-step verification, and it applies to accounts that sign in with a work email and password — organizations using SSO get their second factor from the identity provider instead. Setup offers Authentication App or SMS; the app route issues a standard TOTP secret and, at the end, single-use recovery codes."
  minutes={4}
  params={['6 digits', '30 s period', 'SHA-1']}
  ctaTitle="The host account controls the meeting"
  ctaBody="A hijacked Zoom account can schedule meetings as you, reach cloud recordings, and change account settings. Zoom hands you recovery codes during setup — each works once — so store them and the setup key in your vault before the next call."
  deepDive={{
    heading: 'Zoom account details',
    blocks: [
      {
        title: 'Two-factor authentication protects sign-in, not the meeting',
        body: 'Zoom’s two-step verification guards the account: the web portal, desktop app, mobile app and Zoom Room sign-in. Meeting passcodes, waiting rooms and authentication profiles protect individual meetings. They are separate layers — enabling 2SV does not remove the need for a passcode, and a strict meeting policy does not protect your account.',
      },
      {
        title: 'It covers work-email logins; SSO accounts are handled elsewhere',
        body: 'Zoom states that two-step verification applies to users with the work email (email and password) login type. If your organization signs in through SSO, the identity provider is the place to look for the second factor, not this screen. Admins can also require 2SV for everyone, specific roles or groups, and can reset a user’s setup.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Recovery codes are single-use, and the admin has a reset',
        body: 'Zoom shows a list of recovery codes immediately after you verify the first 6-digit code, and offers Download or Print. Each code works once. If you run out or lose the list, the documented route is Try another way → Recovery codes at sign-in; if that fails, a Zoom admin can reset your two-step verification setup so you can enrol again.',
      },
    ],
  }}
  steps={[
    {
      title: 'Open your Zoom profile',
      body: 'Sign in at zoom.us, click your profile picture or initials in the top-right corner, then “Profile”. The direct shortcut is zoom.us/profile.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Find Two-Step Verification',
      body: 'Under “Sign In”, find “Two-Step Verification”. If it is off, click “Turn On”; if you are already in setup, use “Set Up” to the right of “Authentication App”. Zoom asks for your password at this point.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Choose Authentication App',
      body: 'Zoom offers “Authentication App” or “SMS”. Choose the app, then click “Next” to reach the QR code. Some builds also print a setup key; if yours does not, screenshot the QR — the generator decodes the image on your device.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Generate the code',
      body: 'Paste the setup key into 2facounter.com if you have one; otherwise paste the QR screenshot into the generator and label it “Zoom”. The live 6-digit code refreshes every 30 seconds.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Verify and save the recovery codes',
      body: 'Enter the 6-digit code in Zoom and click “Verify”. Zoom then displays a list of recovery codes — download or print them before clicking “Done”. Each code can be used once, so keep them somewhere you can reach without the phone.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Review account access',
      body: 'Check the “Apps” and “Recordings” sections of the web portal and remove anything unfamiliar. Store the setup secret alongside the recovery codes so a lost phone is an inconvenience, not a lockout.',
    },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'Does Zoom use standard TOTP?',
      a: 'Yes. Zoom’s Authentication App option accepts any app that supports the Time-based One-Time Password protocol, and produces a 6-digit code with SHA-1 and a 30-second period. The same secret works in any compatible authenticator, including this browser generator.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Do meeting attendees need 2FA to join?',
      a: 'No. Two-step verification protects account sign-in, not meeting attendance. Guests join with a meeting link or ID and passcode as usual; only hosts and signed-in users deal with the account’s 2FA.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Why is the authenticator option missing on my Zoom account?',
      a: 'Two-step verification applies to work email (email and password) logins. If your organization enforces SSO, the identity provider handles the second factor instead. Ask your Zoom admin which methods are permitted rather than trying to bypass enforced SSO.',
    },
    {
      q: 'What if I lose my phone and recovery codes?',
      a: 'At sign-in, click “Try another way” → “Recovery codes” and enter one — each works once. If you have none left, ask your Zoom account admin to reset your two-step verification setup; Zoom documents that reset. If you saved the setup secret, the generator on another device keeps producing valid codes, but it does not replace the recovery list for Zoom’s own reset flow.',
    },
  ]}
/>
